logd - the machine's record, out of a serial port

  cfg set log.console ser0     send it out the port `ser` calls ser0
  cfg set log.console ""       send it nowhere, which is the default

This machine has no serial port of its own. That one fact shapes how it
is debugged more than anything else: what it says is read off a
photograph of the screen, or out of the record afterwards with `log`, and
a fault that takes the screen with it leaves neither. A USB serial
adapter gives the machine one.

Name a port and everything the kernel and the services say goes down it:
first the whole record as it stands, so a boot that has already happened
is not lost, and then every line as it is said. `ser` lists the ports and
the name is the one in its first column.

What it cannot carry is the boot before the bus service has found the
adapter, which is the part a serial port on the chip would have had. A
machine that dies before then still says nothing down the wire, and the
panic screen is what that leaves behind.

The far end wants 115200 8N1 unless the port was set to something else,
which `ser <port> set` does and which survives a reboot.

Nothing polls. A machine with no port named waits only to be told the
setting changed; one whose named port is not plugged in waits to be told
the ports changed; one with the console going wakes when the record grows
and when the port has room. Having this running and unused costs one
blocked process and no time at all.

One program to a port, and the last to ask has it, so `ser ser0` while
the console is going takes the port from the console. Naming the port
again, or unplugging and replugging the adapter, puts it back.
